---
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import Cta from '../components/home/Cta.astro';

const rows: [string, string][] = [
  ['Project', 'Project, from a repository on disk or cloned from GitHub'],
  ['Session', 'Task. Starting it creates the worktree and opens a terminal in it.'],
  ['Several sessions from one prompt', 'Several terminals on one task, or ask the agent to split the prompt into tasks.'],
  ['Main branch', 'Branch from, in Project Settings → Worktree, and a merge target per task'],
  ['Run scripts', 'Scripts. Each runs in a runner panel beside the terminal.'],
  ['Diff viewer with manual edits', 'Diff review, with notes the agent reads. Edits happen in your editor, via Open in editor.'],
  ['Rebase from main, Rebase to main', 'From the terminal, or a done hook'],
  ['A commit per iteration', 'None. The agent commits when you ask it to, or a hook does.'],
  ['Prompt history', "The agent's own session, resumed by the continue hook"],
  ['Claude Code and Codex', 'Claude Code, Codex, Pi and OpenCode, with live status and the ouijit command. Any other CLI runs too.'],
  ['Pointing Claude Code at Bedrock', 'The same. The agent is its own CLI, with its own settings.'],
];
---
<Site
  title="Crystal alternative · Ouijit"
  description="Moving from Crystal to Ouijit after its deprecation: how sessions, run scripts, the diff viewer and the rebase buttons map onto tasks, scripts, diff review and hooks.">
  <main class="compare-content">
    <h1>Coming from Crystal</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Crystal was deprecated in February 2026 and its successor is an editor. If what you wanted was Claude Code
      and Codex in parallel worktrees, Ouijit does that, and is maintained. Here's how the pieces line up.
    </p>
    <img
      class="compare-screenshot"
      src="/assets/screenshots/diff.png"
      alt="An agent working in a task's terminal, with the diff of its changes and a note for it beside"
      loading="lazy"
    />

    <h2>Why Ouijit</h2>
    <p>
      The agent runs as its own CLI in a terminal, in the task's worktree, so Claude Code and Codex work exactly as
      they do anywhere else, and Pi and OpenCode join them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every agent gets the <a href="/docs/cli/"><code>ouijit</code> command</a>. Ask it to split a feature into
      tasks and it starts them, each with its own agent. It can read the other agents' terminals, open its plan
      beside itself, link you to the line it changed, and stage review comments for you to send.
    </p>
    <p>
      A task can run in a <a href="/docs/sandbox/">sandbox</a>, so an agent with permission checks off is held to
      its worktree. Ouijit is free, open source, and runs on macOS and Linux.
    </p>

    <h2>What maps to what</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Crystal</th><th>Ouijit</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {rows.map(([a, b]) => (
          <tr><td>{a}</td><td>{b}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>What you give up</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Editing inside the diff. Ouijit's diff is for reading and for notes to the agent.</li>
      <li>A commit after every turn. Commit from the terminal, ask the agent, or put it in a hook.</li>
      <li>The rebase buttons. Git runs in the terminal.</li>
      <li>Windows. Crystal built from source there; Ouijit runs on macOS and Linux.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Move a project over</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Push or finish your Crystal sessions. Ouijit makes its own worktrees.</li>
      <li><a href="/docs/">Install Ouijit</a> and open the repository as a project.</li>
      <li>Under <strong>Project Settings → Hooks</strong>, put your agent in the start hook with the task's prompt, for example <code>claude "$OUIJIT_TASK_DESCRIPTION"</code> or <code>codex "$OUIJIT_TASK_DESCRIPTION"</code>.</li>
      <li>Add each run script as a script.</li>
      <li>Set <strong>Branch from</strong> if your sessions branched from something other than the checked-out branch.</li>
    </ol>
  </main>
  <Cta />
</Site>
